## Why Active Entertainment Works Better for Team Building

Passive activities — trivia nights, seated workshops — put people side by side. Active experiences put them in it together.

When your team is running a laser maze, coordinating a zombie shooter in VR, or competing for the highest score in the Interactive Play rooms, something shifts. Roles flatten. The quiet analyst who never speaks in meetings turns out to be a tactician. The manager who usually leads every conversation has to listen to survive.

Physical, competitive play surfaces different strengths. It builds real rapport faster than any icebreaker exercise ever will.

## What Infusion Play Offers Corporate Groups

Infusion Play (https://infusion-play.com/) runs three distinct experience formats under one roof — which is exactly what makes it work for mixed groups with different energy levels and interests.

### Free-Roaming VR Arena

Strap in and move. The VR Arena runs commercial multiplayer titles including After the Fall and Arizona Sunshine — both zombie shooters that demand real coordination between players. You're not sitting in a chair staring at a screen. You're physically moving through the space with rifle-style controllers, communicating with your team, and making split-second calls together.

This is where competitive energy peaks. Teams rotate through sessions, compare scores, and build genuine bragging rights.

### Interactive Play Rooms

The Interactive Play rooms are where the physical challenge really hits. Run a laser maze without triggering the beams. Sprint, duck, and dodge your way to a high score. Take on spot-the-difference puzzles and Charades-style games that reward sharp thinking and fast communication.

Every session earns points, which feed into a reward redemption system at the end. That built-in scoring mechanic gives your event a natural competitive arc — teams compete, scores accumulate, someone wins. For corporate groups who want friendly rivalry without having to engineer it themselves, that structure does the work for you.

### Claw Machine Lounge

Not everyone wants to sprint through a laser maze right after lunch. The claw machine lounge gives lower-intensity players somewhere to hang out, decompress, and stay engaged. It works well as a warm-up zone before the main sessions or a wind-down spot after — and it keeps the whole group in one place.

